  "summary": "A personal Habit Tracker. Each habit has a name, Category (Health/Learning/Home), Frequency (Daily/Weekly), and a Status (Active by default). Users can add, edit, and delete habits, pause (Active to Paused) and resume them, filter to Active habits only, and see an Active habit count. Data persists to localStorage across refreshes.",
  "implemented_features": [
    "Add a habit with name, Category (Health/Learning/Home), Frequency (Daily/Weekly); Status defaults to Active",
    "Validate required habit name (blank input rejected) and prevent duplicate habit names",
    "Edit an existing habit's name, category, and frequency",
    "Delete a habit",
    "Pause a habit (Active to Paused) and Resume it (Paused to Active)",
    "Filter the collection to Active habits only",
    "Show a derived Active habit count",
    "Persist habits across a browser refresh via localStorage"
  ],
  "assumptions": [
    "The idea lists 'validate required input' without specifying which fields; only the habit name is free text and required, so name is the validated required field. Category and Frequency are constrained dropdowns that always have a value.",
    "Duplicate prevention is implied by 'validate required input' and good UX for repeated add actions; case-insensitive duplicate name detection was implemented to avoid confusing repeated records.",
    "'Filter to Active habits' was implemented as an Active-only filter (the Status the idea centers on). An 'All habits' view is included so paused habits remain accessible, since the idea also requires Resume to reverse Pause.",
    "Persistence uses the browser's localStorage, the only durable single-user store available without a backend. Malformed or duplicate-id stored records are dropped on load so corrupted data does not break the app."
  ],
